; peg.phx -- a grammar written down, and then run.
;
; A dialect, so directives and nothing else. It expects two globals from
; whoever uses it: `rules`, a dictionary of rule name to block, and `src`, a
; cursor from lib/scan.sol.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Why the rules live in a table
;
; The obvious shape is combinators -- a rule is a block, and `seq` and `either`
; are blocks that take blocks and answer blocks. Solveig refuses it:
;
;     makeAdder := { n | { x | x:add(n) } }.
;     makeAdder:value(#5)
;     -> block outlived the frame it was written in
;
; That is 3.1, and lib/scan.sol was pushed the same way for the same reason and
; says so. A block written at the top level and *stored* is fine, because the
; script's frame outlives everything; a block **made inside a call** and
; returned is not. So a rule is a top-level block in a table, and `apply` is a
; lookup rather than a composition.
;
; This was predicted in README.md and confirmed before a line of the toolkit was
; written, which is the only reason it cost nothing.

@use "../../lib/control.phx".

; A rule is a name and a block, and the name is a **symbol literal** rather
; than a bare word. A template cannot build a selector out of a hole -- there
; is no way to turn `<n>` into `rules:<n>` -- so the table takes the name as a
; value. Predicted, and it is the same answer for the same reason.
@syntax rule <n> is <b: block>           => rules:atPut(n, b).
@syntax apply <n>                        => rules:at(n):value.

; What a cursor is asked at a position.
@syntax at <s>                           => src:looksLike(s).
@syntax eat <s>                          => src:match(s).
@syntax next                             => src:next.
@syntax done                             => src:atEnd.
@syntax skip <cls>                       => src:skipWhile({ c | cls:indexOf(c):notNil }).
@syntax take <cls>                       => src:takeWhile({ c | cls:indexOf(c):notNil }).

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Only `<b: block>` is typed, and the first draft typed five more
;
; `skip <cls: literal>` was written that way because every use in the author's
; head was `skip " \t\n"`. The first real grammar wrote `skip blank`, naming a
; string it had bound, and was refused by its own toolkit.
;
; **A kind is a promise extracted from the caller, not a description of the
; author's examples.** `rule` needs a block because the table stores one and
; anything else would store a value; nothing else here needs anything. Typing a
; hole because the uses you have imagined all look alike is how a dialect gets
; narrower than the thing it is for.
